teslamate-rs source packaging notes
===================================

Build system
------------
This package builds with dh-cargo against Debian's packaged crates
(librust-*-dev), not against a rustup toolchain and not by fetching from
crates.io. The build is therefore offline and reproducible, and the
binary gets ${cargo:Built-Using} / ${cargo:Static-Built-Using} as Policy
requires for statically linked Rust.

  sbuild -d unstable

Target suite is unstable. The crate versions this package needs are not
all in trixie.

Source format (for the maintainer)
----------------------------------
debian/source/format is "3.0 (native)" but debian/changelog carries a
Debian revision. dpkg-dev 1.23 warns about this ("native package version
may not have a revision"); older dpkg-dev, including the version in
trixie, refuses to build the source package at all. Either drop the
revision or move to "3.0 (quilt)" with an orig tarball. Left alone here:
it is a release decision, not a build-system one.

Cargo.toml requirements vs Debian
---------------------------------
dh-cargo ignores Cargo.lock entirely and resolves against the installed
librust-*-dev packages, so what matters is the semver requirement in
Cargo.toml, not the pinned version in the lock file.

Debian ships several semver generations of a crate side by side, so most
of teslamate-rs's older pins (rand 0.8, sha2 0.10, aes-gcm 0.10,
argon2 0.5, reqwest 0.12) resolve as-is.

Three requirements needed changing, and have been changed:

  rusqlite            0.32 -> 0.40, and the "bundled" feature dropped.
                      Debian's librust-libsqlite3-sys-dev is a +ds
                      repack with the vendored SQLite amalgamation
                      removed, and links the system libsqlite3 instead.
                      That is the required behaviour for a Debian
                      package - a bundled SQLite copy would not be
                      accepted, and would miss security updates.

  tokio-tungstenite   0.26 -> 0.28. Only src/logger.rs uses it.

  webauthn-rs         Not in Debian at any version, along with its
                      dependencies webauthn-rs-core, webauthn-rs-proto,
                      webauthn-attestation-ca, base64urlsafedata and
                      serde_cbor_2. See "Passkeys" below.

TLS trust store
---------------
Upstream previously selected reqwest's "rustls-tls-webpki-roots"
feature, which compiles a snapshot of the Mozilla CA bundle into the
binary; webpki-roots is also not in Debian. Both reqwest and
tokio-tungstenite now use "rustls-tls-native-roots" instead, and the
package depends on ca-certificates.

Passkeys
--------
webauthn-rs is not packaged in Debian, so the WebAuthn code sits behind
the "passkeys" Cargo feature. It is on by default for upstream builds;
debian/rules passes --no-default-features, so the .deb is built without
it. PAM and password authentication are unaffected; only passkey
enrolment and passkey login are lost.

/api/auth/status reports "webauthn": {"enabled": false} in that build,
and web/auth.js hides the passkey controls when it sees that, so the UI
does not offer routes that are not registered.

Packaging webauthn-rs is the alternative. It needs six new source
packages, and webauthn-rs-core pins asn1-rs 0.6 / der-parser 9 /
x509-parser 0.16 while Debian carries 0.7 / 10 / 0.18, so it would also
need patching to build against the newer parsers. If that lands, drop
CARGO_ARGS from debian/rules and add the librust-webauthn-rs-0.5-dev
build dependency.

Release profile
---------------
[profile.release] no longer sets strip = true, so dh_strip can produce
the teslamate-rs-dbgsym package. Do not reinstate it.

Cargo.lock
----------
dh-cargo deletes Cargo.lock during configure and resolves against
/usr/share/cargo/registry, so the lock file plays no part in the Debian
build. It is stale with respect to the rusqlite and tokio-tungstenite
bumps above and should be regenerated ("cargo update -p rusqlite -p
tokio-tungstenite") for upstream, non-Debian builds.

Build resources
---------------
[profile.release] in Cargo.toml asks for fat LTO with codegen-units = 1.
Combined with the -C debuginfo=2 that Debian needs in order to produce a
dbgsym package, linking the 169 statically linked crates peaks high
enough to get the final rustc OOM-killed on an ordinary machine.

debian/rules therefore sets DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS += optimize=+lto, which
/usr/share/cargo/bin/cargo turns into profile.release.lto="thin". If a
build host is still short of memory, optimize=-lto disables LTO
entirely. Neither changes the upstream profile.

